WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.240
We're already asking the question that's going
to be on everyone's mind once the credits

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.140
roll on Avengers: Endgame: what's next for
the Marvel Cinematic Universe?

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.820
Only time will tell, but like Doctor Strange,
we've looked through 14 million possible futures

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.800
for the MCU and have a few ideas on what we
might see in Phase Four.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.760
Those of you who are slightly less obsessive
about the MCU might be wondering what exactly

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.400
we mean by "Phase Four."

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.260
Basically, while each film walks a delicate
balance between being a standalone feature

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:32.660
and a part of a much larger saga, the MCU
movies are conceptually divided into "phases."

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.930
The idea is that while they might not necessarily
be direct sequels, they each tie into each

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:41.410
other thematically and build to a climax that
unifies everything that came before.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:46.059
Phase One, which began with Iron Man in 2008
and moved through movies like Captain America:

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:50.579
The First Avenger, Thor, and the often overlooked
Incredible Hulk, was all about introducing

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:54.550
each of the superheroes on their own before
bringing them together in The Avengers.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.320
Phase Two was about building a much larger
universe around them, and reframing what a

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.620
superhero was by introducing cosmic heroes
with vast adventures like the Guardians of

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.070
the Galaxy and micro heroes with much smaller
scale adventures, like Ant-Man.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:11.090
Thematically, the climax of Phase Two was
Avengers: Age of Ultron, which also added

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.040
heroes like Vision and the Scarlet Witch to
the team.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.380
And then there's Phase 3, which has been about
loss and about the dire consequences that

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.110
can come from noble intentions.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:24.140
It began with the heroes fighting each other
in Captain America: Civil War and then moved

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:29.260
through several films about heroes discovering
lies and secrets in their past, such as in

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.050
Thor: Ragnarok... and in Black Panther...
and in Captain Marvel.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.420
Now, it's set to reach its endgame in… well,
Endgame.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.360
So what happens in Phase 4?

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.100
The future is shrouded in mystery, but one
thing you can bank on is that the next few

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.470
years of the MCU are going to be focused on
a very different set of characters than the

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.470
last few.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.020
Part of that, of course, is the inevitable
product of the march of time; Chris Evans

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.470
has mentioned that he'd like to be done with
blockbusters and focus on smaller projects,

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:58.750
at least for a while, and Robert Downey Jr.
has expressed some similar desires to move

00:01:58.750 --> 00:01:59.930
on from Tony Stark.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.159
That makes sense, too, because he's been at
this for a while.

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:06.880
Even if you don't count cameos like the one
in Incredible Hulk, and only focus on the

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.469
Iron Man movies and starring roles like the
Avengers films and Spider-Man: Homecoming,

00:02:11.469 --> 00:02:15.239
Downey has played Tony Stark in nine films
over the course of 11 years.

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:18.749
Iron Man might be a character built for a
medium that's meant to go on forever, but

00:02:18.749 --> 00:02:21.600
at some point, you can't blame a guy for wanting
to take a break.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.880
"Okay, anybody on our side hiding any shocking
and fantastic abilities they'd like to disclose,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.180
I'm open to suggestions."

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.419
The flip side to that is that there's some
good news: while Iron Man and Captain America

00:02:34.419 --> 00:02:38.799
were the foundation of the MCU for its first
decade, Phase Three went hard at introducing

00:02:38.799 --> 00:02:40.419
a whole new crop of heroes.

00:02:40.419 --> 00:02:44.329
One of the biggest introductions came when
Marvel and Sony finally reached an agreement

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:49.359
to bring Spider-Man into the MCU, resulting
in one of Marvel's best films in Homecoming.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.420
If Spider-Man was big, though, Black Panther
and Captain Marvel were downright massive.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.680
Both of those films were the first solo outings
for their respective title characters, and

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.659
both racked up over a billion dollars at the
box office.

00:03:00.659 --> 00:03:04.370
Add in Doctor Strange, and the MCU has more
than proven that it can build an entire new

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.140
generation of superhero stars to carry the
torch into Phase 4.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:12.940
While Marvel has said they won't be officially
announcing any Phase 4 projects until after

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:17.930
Spider-Man: Far from Home hits theaters in
the summer of 2019, we do know a few characters

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.450
we're likely to see.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.400
One is Adam Warlock, who was teased at the
end of Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.540
He has traditionally been associated with
the Infinity Stones, so it's unclear what

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.159
role he might play in Phase 4, but it has
been previously announced that he wouldn't

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.340
be seen until Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.
3 comes out.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.960
Then there's The Eternals.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.799
Created by the great Jack Kirby, The Eternals
is set to star Angelina Jolie and features

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:45.449
a group of humans who long ago were experimented
on and given tremendous powers by the cosmic

00:03:45.449 --> 00:03:50.409
space gods known as The Celestials, who previously
cameoed in Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:54.620
And finally, there's Shang-Chi, the Master
of Kung Fu, who is the world's greatest martial

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:55.220
artist.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.239
In the comics, Shang-Chi dedicated his life
to defeating the world-conquering plans of

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.790
his father, an immortal Chinese villain.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:06.219
The announced MCU film is reportedly going
to be updated for modern audiences, though,

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:11.780
so just what role Shang-Chi, or any of these
new faces, will play in Phase 4 is still unclear.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:16.940
One idea that has only been touched on briefly
in the films is the concept of the legacy

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:17.950
character.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.379
In the comics, a legacy character is one where
new characters take over existing superhero

00:04:22.379 --> 00:04:26.560
identities once the original hero has moved
on via retirement or death.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.990
Jim Rhodes was the second Iron Man, and both
Sam Wilson and Bucky Barnes have spent time

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.260
as Captain America.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:33.980
"You couldn't have done that earlier?"

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.180
"I hate you."

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.920
So far, the only hint of this in the MCU has
been with Ant-Man and the Wasp, with Scott

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.650
Lang and Hope van Dyne taking over those identities
from Hank Pym and Janet van Dyne.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.860
But if Chris Evans and Robert Downey Jr. do
indeed hang up their spandex after Avengers:

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.570
Endgame, it's very possible we may see Rhodey
take over as Iron Man, or Bucky take over

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.090
as Captain America.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:00.280
After all, what better way to pay homage to
the founders of the MCU than by making sure

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:01.800
their names live on forever?

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.939
One safe assumption to make about the MCU
going forward is that whatever storyline weaves

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:11.080
through and unites the next 20 movies, it
won't be focused on Thanos and the Infinity

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.080
Stones.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.710
Once you gather up the six of them, forge
them into a giant golden gauntlet, and kill

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:19.030
half of the universe, after all, there's really
no way to top it.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:23.280
There's a reason beyond just a logical progression
of events that leads us to think we're going

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.090
to be done with the stones, though.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:30.060
Marvel Studios President of Production Kevin
Feige collectively referred to the first 22

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.400
MCU films as "The Infinity Saga."

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.360
In addition to being a nice designation to
fit on the side of a ridiculously expensive

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.479
box set of Blu-rays, that's a pretty clear
indication that the stories built around the

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:42.219
Stones are coming to an end.

00:05:42.219 --> 00:05:44.710
So what will the next MCU Saga be about?

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.880
Luckily, it turns out that there are plenty
of possibilities.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.840
One of the things that makes it so difficult
to figure out where the Marvel movies are

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:55.860
going after Endgame is that eliminating the
Infinity Stones doesn't really narrow down

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.409
our options for what might take their place.

00:05:58.409 --> 00:06:02.550
In the comic book pages, Marvel has spent
about 60 years creating a nearly endless supply

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:03.870
of MacGuffins.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.830
If you're not familiar with the term, a "MacGuffin"
is a term for a plot device that sparks the

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:08.830
inciting incident.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.940
It's the thing that everyone's looking for,
like the briefcase in Pulp Fiction or the

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.020
Maltese Falcon in The Maltese Falcon.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.969
Or like the six magic stones that everyone
in the MCU has been obsessing about for the

00:06:18.969 --> 00:06:20.060
last decade.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:24.389
Luckily, if Marvel decides to go the MacGuffin
route again, there are plenty of candidates.

00:06:24.389 --> 00:06:28.389
For instance, the Darkhold, which is the user
manual for black magic and which appeared

00:06:28.389 --> 00:06:33.069
in season 4 of Agents of SHIELD, would be
a natural starting point for a saga involving

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:34.069
Dormammu.

00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:38.039
The Casket of Ancient Winters, which briefly
appeared in the first Thor film, contains

00:06:38.039 --> 00:06:39.999
the infinite power of nature.

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:44.770
Then there's the Evil Eye of Avalon, the Nega-Bands,
U.S. Archer's trailer truck… the list is

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:45.900
nearly endless.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.830
With so many to choose from, it will probably
go down to which best fits the bad guys they

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:50.980
want to use.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:55.460
The Marvel Cinematic Universe has had more
than its fair share of incredibly powerful

00:06:55.469 --> 00:06:59.270
supervillains, from Loki and Hela to Ultron
and Ego, the Living Planet.

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:03.350
But that's the nice thing about superheroes:
they never seem to run out of villains.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:08.009
If Marvel decides to bring in another cosmic
threat like Thanos, Annihilus is one obvious

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:08.620
candidate.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:12.740
There are also potentially more interesting
directions they could take, such as the time

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.310
traveling warlord Kang the Conqueror, or the
world eating Galactus.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.010
Or they could go with a more mystical threat,
such as Dormammu.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.430
When it comes right down to it, there's really
just one big villain that stands out above

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.430
the rest.

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:27.140
The only thing is, though, he comes with some
baggage...

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:31.900
Probably the most exciting aspect of Phase
Four is the possibility that Marvel could

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:37.159
finally introduce MCU versions of some of
their most beloved heroes and villains, including

00:07:37.159 --> 00:07:40.069
the Fantastic Four and their archenemy, Doctor
Doom.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:42.979
Of course, these characters have appeared
on film before.

00:07:42.979 --> 00:07:44.250
Three times, in fact.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.789
But you can be forgiven for forgetting that,
as most fans of the characters have tried

00:07:47.789 --> 00:07:51.719
to convince themselves for years that those
movies were all just a bad dream.

00:07:51.719 --> 00:07:54.779
The three previous Fantastic Four films were
made by Fox.

00:07:54.779 --> 00:07:59.289
Now that Disney has purchased Fox, however,
the rights have reverted to Marvel Studios,

00:07:59.289 --> 00:08:02.199
giving them a chance to finally get these
characters right.

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:06.150
And as we saw with Spider-Man; Homecoming,
nobody knows how to do Marvel characters like

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.379
Marvel themselves.

00:08:07.379 --> 00:08:11.400
As exciting as it will be to finally see the
Fantastic Four done well, though, it's almost

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:16.129
more exciting to consider MCU versions of
some of their supporting cast, including the

00:08:16.129 --> 00:08:20.680
enigmatic Silver Surfer and Marvel's number
one supervillain, Doctor Doom.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:25.229
After all, if Marvel can turn a talking tree
into a worldwide pop culture icon, imagine

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:27.840
what they can do with the coolest villain
in comics.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:32.880
For a lot of fans, the Fantastic Four pale
in comparison to the big acquisition from

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.039
the Disney/Fox deal: the X-Men.

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:38.970
On their own, they're a franchise that's about
as massive and complicated as the rest of

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.180
the Marvel Universe put together, and they
also have their own sprawling, mostly successful

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:44.290
film franchise.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:48.459
The MCU so far has gone out of its way to
carve out a world without mutants, to the

00:08:48.459 --> 00:08:52.899
point of referring to Quicksilver and the
Scarlet Witch by a similar but legally distinct

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:56.280
M-word when they showed up at the end of Captain
America: The Winter Soldier.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:58.980
"This is the age of miracles, doctor.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:05.840
There is nothing more horrifying than a miracle."

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:09.620
Now that Marvel has the rights to the X-Men
again, though, they are free to introduce

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.410
as many mutants as they want.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:15.381
Or, not; as we've learned from superhero films,
there are an infinite number of ways you can

00:09:15.381 --> 00:09:20.170
get superpowers, so it's possible in the MCU,
the X-Men won't be mutants at all.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:24.529
And if the X-Men do show up in the MCU during
Phase Four, they might just wind up being

00:09:24.529 --> 00:09:28.290
used much more sparingly than they have been
in their own film series.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:32.279
If nothing else, it would allow fans who were
still attached to the Fox X-Men series to

00:09:32.279 --> 00:09:36.690
have some breathing room before reintroducing
characters like Storm or Jean Grey.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.839
Whatever Marvel does in Phase 4, though, one
thing is guaranteed: we'll be first in line

00:09:40.839 --> 00:09:42.500
at the theater to find out.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:43.420
See you there!

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.000
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.910
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
Marvel heroes are coming soon.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:55.320
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

